Each corner becomes a hashed lookup

Now the key trick.

A corner is a 2D integer coordinate:

v=(i,j)

But we do NOT store features for every possible (i,j), because that would be enormous.

Instead: Hash function

We map the coordinate into a table index:

h(i,j)=((i⋅π1)⊕(j⋅π2))modT

where:

T = size of hash table (e.g. 2¹⁹ ≈ 524k slots)
⊕ - XOR
π1​,π2 are constants
π1​=1
π2​=2654435761
Result:

index∈[0,T−1]


(23,46) → hash → 381921
so table[k]∈RF, where F is dimension. in this case F = 2.
table[381921] = [0.12, -0.44] (at beginn random values which are trained)

This is NOT computed — it is learned.

Do it for 4 corners: 

corner (i,j)=(5,11)
corner (i+1,j) = (6, 11)
corner (i,j + 1) = (5, 12)
corner(i+1, j+1) = (6, 12)

f00 = learned feature vector of dimension f
like f00 = [0.12, -0.44]

f00	​=Table[hash(5,11)]
f10	​=Table[hash(6,11)]

v00 → f00
v10 → f10
v01 → f01
v11 → f11


Trainable weights: 
table[381921] = [0.3,  0.7]
table[74823]  = [0.5,  0.2]
table[209134] = [0.1,  0.9]
table[512001] = [0.8,  0.4]

